{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1442357080,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUxMDY2NjMwNjE=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/210","origin":"github"},"title":"bash/zsh completions","message":"I guess we could do it manually but quick search brought me to  https://argcomplete.readthedocs.org which seems to be quite neat:\nfollowing minimal intrusion:\n\n```\n$\u003e git diff\ndiff --git a/datalad/cmdline/main.py b/datalad/cmdline/main.py\nindex 5c4c88a..5e5de9a 100644\n--- a/datalad/cmdline/main.py\n+++ b/datalad/cmdline/main.py\n@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@\n\n __docformat__ = 'restructuredtext'\n\n-\n+import argcomplete\n import argparse\n import logging\n import sys\n@@ -172,6 +172,7 @@ def generate_api_call(cmdlineargs=None):\n\n def main(cmdlineargs=None):\n     parser = setup_parser()\n+    argcomplete.autocomplete(parser)\n     # parse cmd args\n     cmdlineargs = parser.parse_args(cmdlineargs)\n     # run the function associated with the selected command\n```\n\npros: allows to define extended completions (e.g. handles, collections) within python code itself.  Should generally be more homogeneous with the rest\n\ncons: additional 3rd party dependence (present in Debian/ubuntus), requires its own installation within completions system","files":null}]}